  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble model guidance has come into better agreement on the \nsynoptic pattern for the beginning of the workweek. Ensemble members \nshow the longwave trough over Western CONUS lifting northeastward \nand pushing into the Northern Rockies, TC Marie moving \nnorth/northwestward (staying well off the coast of California), and \nthe subtropical ridge pushing back into the Desert Southwest. With \nthe subtropical ridge building back overhead, H5 heights will rise \nto around 591-594 dm. This increase in heights aloft will result in \na gradual warming trend. The NBM shows afternoon high temperatures \nincreasing to 100-108 degrees across the lower deserts and 90-100 \ndegrees across the higher terrain. Temperatures of this magnitude \nare slightly above normal, which will result in widespread \nModerate HeatRisk across the lower deserts. The aforementioned \nsynoptic pattern will also result in the continuation of dry \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. PWATs are expected to drop to around \n1.3-1.6\", resulting in mostly dry conditions across the CWA. \n\nHeading into the later half of the workweek, ensemble model guidance \ncontinue to disagree on the synoptic pattern. Not only do the models \ndiffer on the strength and positioning of the subtropical ridge, \nthey also differ on the strength and positioning of the low pressure \nsystem over the eastern Pacific. Overall the EPS has a stronger \nsolution, it is showing a stronger subtropical ridge along with a \nstronger/deeper low pressure system in the Pacific (in fact it is \nshowing a closed low). Additionally, the EPS has both of these \nfeatures further west. The EPS shows the high maxing out around 597 \ndm during the middle of the week, with the center stretched across \nthe Plains, and then the high weakening slightly, but moving \noverhead by the end of the workweek. Then the GEFS is showing a \nweaker ridge and a weaker, open wave trough, with both of these \nfeatures further east. These difference can be seen in the large \ntemperature spreads in the NBM interquartile range (with the EPS \nbeing a warmer solution and the GEFS being a cooler solution). The \nNBM continues to lean more towards the GEFS, with a gradual cooling \ntrend beginning Wednesday. Both ensemble models do show a return of \nmoisture by the middle of next week, with PWATs increasing to around \n1.5-1.8\" (EPS has slightly higher PWATs). This increase in\nmoisture will result in rain chances returning to the region as \nearly as Wednesday. The NBM shows PoPs of 20-40% area wide by the \nWednesday- Thursday timeframe. The GFS also shows an inverted \ntrough moving through the region during the middle of next week. \nIf this were to occur it would significantly increase rain \nchances for the region. Despite this, both the GEFS and EPS do \nshow measurable rainfall returning to at least some portions of \nthe region during the latter half of the upcoming workweek. \nHowever, forecast confidence remains low and we'll have to wait to\nsee how the models evolve over the coming days.",
